                        "language": "english",
                        "review": "[h2]No Story. No Bullsh*t. Just Shoot.[/h2]\nTotal f*cking hidden gem! I enjoyed every second of this game.\n\nIt's insanely simple, just like its title. You stand, shoot and RELOAD! Sometimes you move after clearing a particular area, but most of the time you're just standing there, blasting everything that appears. And honestly, that simplicity is exactly what I love about it. It just blesses my soul.\n\nThis game is pure gameplay and nothing else. Unlike the Heavy Fire games, which try to force some half-baked story into the experience, Reload doesn't bother with any of that. You're here, you shoot, and that's it.\n\nThe graphics are also much better than in Heavy Fire. Everything in Reload is clear and easy to see, while Heavy Fire tries way too hard to look cinematic with overly dramatic shadows and blinding sunlight. It doesn't add anything to the experience - it just gets in the way. Reload keeps everything clean and readable, and it simply makes more sense as an arcade shooter.\n\nI especially loved the bonus levels where you shoot fruit, cans, bottles, and other random targets. They're incredibly fun and add to the game's old-school arcade feel. It's just a genuinely fun game to kill some time with, and it honestly made me happy.\n\nIf you're going for all the achievements, though, I ran into one annoying issue with the Rifle Expert achievement, which requires earning a Platinum Medal on the Basic Reflexive Fire mission. I couldn't figure out how anyone was supposed to reach 30,000 points. It turns out you have to shoot every target called out by the instructor's voice to earn bonus points after each wave. Those bonuses are what push you over the 30,000-point requirement. The game never makes this scoring mechanic obvious, and this mission works differently from the others, so it's pretty confusing.\n\nThat's really my only complaint. Other than that, I had fun from start to finish.\n[h2]7/10 - An absolute hidden gem that's all about simple, satisfying gameplay with no unnecessary fluff. If you just want to stand, shoot, and have fun, Reload absolutely delivers.[/h2]",
